==Trivia==
*Each map is named after a different breed of cat, though it's only obvious when they're written in Japanese:
**[[Hidden Forest of Gapra|Gapra]] (ガープラ) = [[wikipedia:Singapura cat|Singapura]] (シン<u>ガプーラ</u>)
**[[Ashvini Desert|Ashvini]] (アシビニ) = [[wikipedia:Abyssinian cat|Abyssinian]] (<u>アビシニ</u>アン)
**Janfore (ジャンフォレ) ([[Jinfore Volcano|Jinfore]]'s name in [[Nyanko Daisensou|BCJP]]) = [[wikipedia:Norwegian Forest cat|Norwegian Forest Cat]] (ノルウェー<u>ジャンフォレ</u>ストキャット)
*Like the Behemoths themselves, a few of these stages make references to ''Monster Hunter'':
**The most likely reason why the final boss of the Behemoth Culling stages is an [[Ensanglion the Calamity|enemy counterpart]] of [[Ganglion (Uber Rare Cat)|Ganglion]] is that he's an [[wikia:monsterhunter:Elder Dragon|Elder Dragon]], just like most of the "final bosses" of the ''Monster Hunter'' games.
**[[Venomous Duck]]'s Japanese name, Mōdoku Kaichō (猛毒怪鳥, "Deadly Poisonous Strange Bird), contains [[wikia:monsterhunter:Gypceros|Gypceros]]' Japanese title: Doku Kaichō (毒怪鳥, "Poison Strange Bird").
**[[Performant Thunderclap]]'s Japanese name, Kanaderu wa Jinrai no Oto (奏でるは迅雷の音, "Playing is the Sound of Thunderclaps"), is similar to the title of one of the quests in  ''[[wikia:monsterhunter:Monster Hunter Portable 3rd|Monster Hunter Portable 3rd]]'': Mau wa Arashi, Kanaderu wa Saika no Shirabe (舞うは嵐、奏でるは災禍の調べ, "Dancing is the Storm, Playing is the Melody of Disaster"). This quest requires the player to hunt an [[wikia:monsterhunter:Amatsu|Amatsu]].
*The names of the stages within the Oddity in the Desert, Affliction in the Forest and Peril in the Volcano maps correlate with the Behemoths appearing in them:
**[[The Strongest Behemoth]]'s Japanese name, Saikyō no Mōjū (最強の猛獣, "The Strongest Fierce Beast"), is also present in the Japanese descriptions of [[THE FOLIVOREAN]] and its original variant, [[THE SLOTH]]. Although the stage's English name translates it as "Behemoth", 猛獣 ''Mōjū'' ("Fierce Beast") is different from the [[:Category:Behemoth Enemies|Behemoth]] enemy type's Japanese name (超獣 ''Chōjū'', "Super-Beast").
**Venomous Duck introduces [[Casaurian Darkjo]].
**[[Two Great Shadows]] has two main threats. Casaurian Darkjo is [[:Category:Dark Enemies|Dark]] as a shadow, while THE FOLIVOREAN turning into an [[:Category:Aku Enemies|Aku]] could be likened to it becoming a shadow of its former self, as after deforestation evicted it from its original habitat it became unaccustomed to its new environment and went on a violent rampage.
**[[Tip of the Beast's Tongue]] introduces [[Vermilingua Rangmaster]], who attacks with a literal tongue-lashing.
**[[Earthshaking Fist]] contains [[Ragin' Gory]] and [[Angelic Beast Rajakong]], who shake the earth with their fists.
**Performant Thunderclap might reference the electricity that crackles around Vermilingua Rangmaster's tongue.
**[[Sharp-Toothed Death Fish]] refers to [[Lophiiformes Angaburu]].
**[[Angry Explosion]] combines the angry [[Elephantidae Papaou]] and Ragin' Gory with the explosive [[Formicidaean Ariant]].
**[[Cursed Flaming Party]] pairs the curse-inflicting [[Ancient Magamojoe]] with the fiery [[Infernal Pegasus Deonil]].
*Whenever the Behemoth Culling maps were extended, [[PONOS Corporation|PONOS]] always added one more stage than was available in-game. The inaccessible stages were always placeholders with no rewards and an unbuffed [[Doge]] as the only enemy until it was time for them to be implemented proper.

